.TH SIMPLE_SHELL 1 "August 2026" "simple_shell 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
simple_shell \- a simple UNIX command line interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B simple_shell
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B simple_shell
is a minimalist replica of the standard UNIX shell (sh).
It reads a line of input from the user, either interactively
from a terminal or from a non-interactive stream (for example
a pipe or a script passed on standard input), splits it into
a command and its arguments, and executes it.
.PP
When running interactively,
.B simple_shell
displays a prompt showing the last two components of the
current working directory, followed by
.BR "$ " ,
before reading each line of input.
When input is not a terminal, no prompt is printed.
.PP
For each line entered, the shell:
.IP \(bu 3
Checks whether the line matches one of the built-in commands
.RB ( "exit" " or " "env" ).
.IP \(bu 3
Otherwise splits the line into tokens (separated by spaces, tabs,
carriage returns, newlines, or the alert character) to build an
argument vector.
.IP \(bu 3
Searches for the command in the directories listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable (or uses the command as-is if it contains a
.RB \(dq / \(dq ).
.IP \(bu 3
Forks a child process and executes the command via
.BR execve (2),
waiting for it to complete before returning to the prompt.
.SH BUILT-IN COMMANDS
.TP
.B exit
Exits the shell, returning the exit status of the last command
executed.
.TP
.B env
Prints the current environment, one variable per line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B simple_shell
exits with the status of the last command it executed.
If a command cannot be found in the
.B PATH
or is not executable, the shell prints an error message of the form
.RS
.I shell_name: line_number: command: not found
.RE
to standard error and sets the exit status to 127, matching the
convention used by
.BR sh (1).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
Colon-separated list of directories searched, in order, for the
executable named in the command. If a command name contains a
.RB \(dq / \(dq
character, it is treated as a path and executed directly instead
of being looked up in
.BR PATH .
.SH EXAMPLES
Start the shell interactively:
.RS
.nf
$ ./simple_shell
~/home/user$ ls -l
~/home/user$ env
~/home/user$ exit
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a script of commands non-interactively:
.RS
.nf
$ echo "ls -l" | ./simple_shell
.fi
.RE
.SH BUGS
.B simple_shell
does not support pipes, redirection, logical operators
.RB ( && ", " || ", " ; ),
comments, or command substitution. Only one command per line is
supported.
.SH AUTHOR
Written by Jean De Foucault-Lebel and Bryce Vermorel as a training project
reproducing the essential behavior of a UNIX shell.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR execve (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR wait (2)
